    "id": "fluid-moody-chart",
    "title": "Moody Chart",
    "field": "Fluid Dynamics / Aerodynamics",
    "plotType": "Log-log family of curves (friction factor vs. Reynolds number)",
    "description": "The Moody chart plots the Darcy friction factor against Reynolds number on log-log axes, with a family of curves for different pipe relative roughness values. It compresses the entire empirical relationship for pressure loss in pipe flow into one reference chart: a steep laminar line, a transition zone, then roughness curves that flatten into fully-rough plateaus at high Reynolds number. It is the workhorse of piping and duct design for computing head loss.",
    "dataNeeded": [
      {
        "name": "Reynolds number Re",
        "unit": "unitless"
      },
      {
        "name": "Relative roughness (e/D)",
        "unit": "ratio"
      },
      {
        "name": "Darcy friction factor f",
        "unit": "unitless"
      }
    ],
    "whatItTellsYou": "In the laminar region friction factor falls linearly with Re independent of roughness; in the turbulent region rougher pipes ride higher, more friction-prone curves. Where a roughness curve flattens, flow is fully rough and friction no longer depends on Re. Reading the friction factor lets you compute pumping-pressure loss for a given pipe and flow rate.",
